WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.196
<v Human Services Supervisor>I want you to lead the provider staffing guide. You

00:00:03.196 --> 00:00:06.072
<v Human Services Supervisor>know these providers better than anyone on the team.

00:00:06.422 --> 00:00:09.516
<v Senior Program Consultant>I would like that. Can I ask what leading means

00:00:09.516 --> 00:00:12.611
<v Senior Program Consultant>here? On my last team, leading meant writing a draft

00:00:12.611 --> 00:00:15.086
<v Senior Program Consultant>and then waiting for edits on every page.

00:00:15.436 --> 00:00:18.947
<v Human Services Supervisor>Fair question. Let's sort it out now. You decide the

00:00:18.947 --> 00:00:22.457
<v Human Services Supervisor>structure, the wording and who tests it with you. Legal

00:00:22.457 --> 00:00:25.968
<v Human Services Supervisor>review stays with the person or area responsible for it.

00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:29.479
<v Human Services Supervisor>The release date stays with me, because it ties to

00:00:29.479 --> 00:00:30.532
<v Human Services Supervisor>the rule change.

00:00:30.882 --> 00:00:33.430
<v Senior Program Consultant>And if I want to add a section on common

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:34.194
<v Senior Program Consultant>mistakes providers make?

00:00:34.544 --> 00:00:37.934
<v Human Services Supervisor>That is yours. Talk to me first only if a

00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:41.324
<v Human Services Supervisor>choice changes what providers are required to do. Then it

00:00:41.324 --> 00:00:42.680
<v Human Services Supervisor>needs a policy check.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:46.430
<v Senior Program Consultant>That helps. Honestly, my review queue is already long. I

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.150
<v Senior Program Consultant>was going to fit this in at night.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.333
<v Human Services Supervisor>Please don't. Research on empowering leadership finds that the same

00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:57.166
<v Human Services Supervisor>trust that raises confidence can also raise strain, and strain

00:00:57.166 --> 00:01:00.999
<v Human Services Supervisor>eats the gain. Which four reviews can move to someone

00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:02.532
<v Human Services Supervisor>else for six weeks?

00:01:02.882 --> 00:01:06.105
<v Senior Program Consultant>The two day-service reviews and the two renewals that are

00:01:06.105 --> 00:01:07.394
<v Senior Program Consultant>not due until spring.

00:01:07.744 --> 00:01:10.765
<v Human Services Supervisor>Done. I will move them today. What else do you

00:01:10.765 --> 00:01:12.880
<v Human Services Supervisor>need to make good decisions on this?

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.562
<v Senior Program Consultant>Time with the policy lead who wrote the rule summary,

00:01:16.562 --> 00:01:18.894
<v Senior Program Consultant>and the call-line notes on provider questions.

00:01:19.244 --> 00:01:22.497
<v Human Services Supervisor>I will introduce you to the policy lead this week

00:01:22.497 --> 00:01:25.751
<v Human Services Supervisor>and ask the call-line supervisor to share the notes. Let's

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:29.004
<v Human Services Supervisor>check in once, when you have a first draft. Call

00:01:29.004 --> 00:01:30.956
<v Human Services Supervisor>me sooner if something touches requirements.

00:01:31.306 --> 00:01:34.074
<v Senior Program Consultant>One more thing. If you would have done the structure

00:01:34.074 --> 00:01:35.458
<v Senior Program Consultant>differently, will you change it?

00:01:35.808 --> 00:01:39.145
<v Human Services Supervisor>I will tell you what I would have done and

00:01:39.145 --> 00:01:42.483
<v Human Services Supervisor>ask what you weighed. I will only reverse something that

00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:45.820
<v Human Services Supervisor>puts people served, the law or policy at risk. Otherwise,

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:46.488
<v Human Services Supervisor>it stands.
